---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '@/data/site';
import updated from '@/data/updated.json';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility"
  description={`How ${site.name}'s portfolio is built and tested for accessibility: the WCAG 2.2 AA target, what is tested, and known limits.`}
>
  <article class="wrap a11y-statement prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Accessibility statement</p>
    <h1>Accessibility</h1>
    <p>
      This site aims to meet <strong>WCAG 2.2 level AA</strong>. Accessibility is part of what I build for a
      living, so it is tested like any other behaviour, and the gaps are listed here rather than hidden.
    </p>

    <h2>What is tested automatically, on every change</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Automated WCAG 2.2 A and AA checks (axe) on every page, in light and dark themes, healthy and broken
        states, across 10 device profiles in Chromium, WebKit and Firefox.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keyboard-only use: a skip link, visible focus that is never hidden behind the sticky header or the
        health bar, and a data table with one Tab stop and arrow-key navigation.
      </li>
      <li>Reduced motion: animations, page transitions and the page-freezing demos are turned off.</li>
      <li>
        Text spacing overrides (WCAG 1.4.12) clip nothing, pages work at a 320 px width without sideways
        scrolling, and controls have targets of at least 24 px.
      </li>
      <li>Without JavaScript, the case studies, résumé and contact details are all still readable.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Known limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Screen-reader testing with VoiceOver, NVDA and TalkBack is not yet complete. Automated checks cannot
        replace it.
      </li>
      <li>
        The demos re-create real problems on purpose: one freezes the page for a second or two, and one shows
        raw translation keys. They only do this when you ask them to, and the freezing ones are skipped if you
        prefer reduced motion.
      </li>
      <li>
        The accessibility demo's numbers come from three automated checks on one panel. They illustrate a fix;
        they are not a conformance audit.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Found a problem?</h2>
    <p>
      Please tell me at <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. I treat accessibility problems as
      bugs.
    </p>
    <p class="a11y-statement__date">Last reviewed {updated.site}.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .a11y-statement {
    padding-block: var(--space-16);
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 3vw, 3.4rem);
  }

  .a11y-statement__date {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
